    "pressure": {
      "file": "sewage_lift_station_pressure.glb",
      "title": "Lifting sewage to a higher sewer",
      "description": "Gravity sewers get deeper downstream; a lift station raises the flow into a rising (force) main or a higher sewer. Pumps start and stop on level so they do not cycle too often (typically ≤ 6–10 starts/h per motor — wet-well volume is sized for it) and the duty alternates. Non-clog impellers pass solids (often ≥ 75–100 mm); rising-main velocity is kept ≈ 0.7–2.5 m/s to avoid settling. Pumps lift out on the guide rails without anyone entering the well — a confined space with H₂S risk.",
      "render": "normal",
      "annotations": []
    }
